// Calling `main` must be a clean positioned error, not raw LLVM verifier
// output. It used to emit:
//
//     Module verification failed: Incorrect number of arguments passed to
//     called function!  %call = call i32 @main()
//
// `main` is the process ENTRY POINT: codegen gives it the C signature — an i32
// return plus (argc, argv) — so a Goo-level call emits a 0-argument call to a
// 2-parameter function. That is exactly the backend noise this harness forbids
// reaching users (see its global verifier-noise assertion).
//
// REJECTED rather than supported, deliberately. Go permits calling main.
// Passing dummy argc/argv instead would be WORSE than failing: the entry
// prologue re-runs goo_os_args_init on every call, so a recursive call would
// silently wipe os.Args — a quiet wrong answer replacing a loud one. Supporting
// it properly means emitting the body under a non-entry symbol with the C main
// as a thin wrapper, which changes emission for every program.
package main

import "fmt"

func helper() {
	main()
}

func main() {
	fmt.Println("hi")
	helper()
}
